WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.470
Test one change, prove the result, and plan your next 90 days.

2
00:00:04.820 --> 00:00:09.850
This is the video for Module 4 of Productivity and Time Management at Work.

3
00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:22.110
Saying I am very organised is a claim. Showing that you cut interruptions from 23 to 7 a day is evidence. Employers, clients and partners trust evidence.

4
00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:32.210
In this module you test one change against your baseline, present the result, and write a 90-day plan so the new habits last beyond the course.

5
00:00:33.460 --> 00:00:40.310
Look at your baseline and the biggest time drain you named. Choose the one method from this course most likely to fix it.

6
00:00:41.560 --> 00:00:55.040
Write a hypothesis, a prediction you can check. If I make this change, then this measure will move from my baseline to my target within two weeks. Write it before you start, so you cannot quietly change the target later.

7
00:00:56.290 --> 00:00:59.900
Set everything up on day 0, and tell the people affected.

8
00:01:01.150 --> 00:01:05.890
Track the same measures in the same way as your baseline, with a short daily note.

9
00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:14.220
Then compare averages, not your best day. Note anything unusual, like a public holiday or a power outage.

10
00:01:15.470 --> 00:01:21.890
The common mistake is adding a second change in the middle. Write new ideas on your someday list for later.

11
00:01:23.140 --> 00:01:38.050
Rukayat's baseline showed 6 meetings a day, spread from 9 to 4. Her hypothesis: if I move meetings to 1 to 4, and block 9 to 11:30 for deep work, my focused hours will rise to at least 2 a day.

12
00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:48.840
In week 1, two client meetings still land in the morning, so she offers afternoon slots. Over two weeks, focused hours average 1.9 a day.

13
00:01:50.090 --> 00:01:58.710
Her weekly priorities rise from 2 of 5 to 4 of 5. She keeps the change, and adjusts it to allow client mornings once a week.

14
00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:04.730
And if your numbers do not improve, that is still evidence. Write why, and what you would adjust.

15
00:02:05.980 --> 00:02:15.960
Now turn the experiment into a one-page report. Start with the problem and your baseline numbers. Then the one change you tested, and a before-and-after table.

16
00:02:17.210 --> 00:02:34.150
Then value. Managers think in results and money. If each extra sales visit brings one order in four, and an order earns about 10000 naira profit, 4 extra visits a week is roughly one extra order. Always label estimates as estimates.

17
00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:40.350
End with your next step, and any request, like a team agreement on message times.

18
00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:44.160
A claim with no numbers is easy to ignore.

19
00:02:45.410 --> 00:02:59.860
Bukola, the Lagos caterer, writes a CV line instead. She redesigned customer messaging with WhatsApp Business quick replies, reducing daily interruptions by about 80 percent while keeping same-day replies.

20
00:03:00.210 --> 00:03:08.670
In interviews, use STAR: the situation, the task, the action you took, and the result with numbers. Practise it in under two minutes.

21
00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:23.360
Report your real numbers, including what did not improve. Leave colleagues' names and client details out of anything you share outside your organisation, and ask your manager before posting work data publicly.

22
00:03:24.610 --> 00:03:30.350
Nearly 2 hours is fine when your log says 1.9. But do not round in your own favour.

23
00:03:31.600 --> 00:03:42.970
90 days matches a business quarter, the same period as an OKR. Score your Module 1 OKR from 0 to 1 for each key result, then write a sharper new one.

24
00:03:43.320 --> 00:03:46.800
Name your next biggest time drain and the change you will test.

25
00:03:47.150 --> 00:03:56.770
Put your daily plan, deep-work block, message batches, shutdown routine and weekly review into Google Calendar as repeating events, not intentions.

26
00:03:57.120 --> 00:04:04.380
And plan for slips. If I miss my weekly review two weeks in a row, I will do a 20-minute version on Monday morning.

27
00:04:05.630 --> 00:04:19.660
To remember. Test one change against your baseline, with a hypothesis written first. Show evidence, not claims, and label estimates. Schedule your keeper habits, and decide now how you will restart when they slip.

28
00:04:20.910 --> 00:04:31.860
Now your turn. Launch your two-week experiment, write your one-page report, and write your 90-day plan. Save all your evidence in one Google Drive folder as you go.

29
00:04:32.210 --> 00:04:34.150
Continue with the lessons below.
